++++## 1.3) Macros
++++
++++Default to **plain SQL**, not a macro. A Jinja macro trades a little less repetition for worse readability and debuggability — the real logic isn't visible in one place, and errors surface in generated SQL that no source file contains. So the bar is high: **a macro must give value that plain SQL or a model cannot, and that value must outweigh the readability cost.** If it doesn't, write the SQL.
++++
++++**Do not use a macro to:**
++++
++++- **Hold a static list** (e.g. "the metrics in the benchmark"). Put the list where it is used. If two models need the same list, have one model build the result and the other read it (the COMPOSE rule below) — that keeps them in sync without a macro.
++++- **Wrap a metric formula for DRY.** Compute the metric once in an `int_*` model; downstream marts **select** that result, they do not recompute it. Reusing a rollup is clearer than regenerating the same SQL in two places.
++++- **Scaffold for a policy or feature that is not approved and live yet.** Build the helper when the thing ships, not before — an unused macro is cost (maintenance, confusion) with no value until then.
++++
++++**A macro is justified when:**
++++
++++- **dbt requires it** — framework hooks such as `generate_schema_name`.
++++- **It is a genuinely cross-cutting transform that cannot be a model** and would otherwise be copied, error-prone, into many files — e.g. parsing a raw provider JSON payload in staging.
++++
++++**The COMPOSE rule.** One model produces a result; others read from it. Never recompute the same thing in two places, and never reach for a macro just to keep two copies in sync — collapse them into one model instead.
++++
++++**The test before adding a macro:** *"Does this give value that plain SQL or a model cannot?"* If the answer is no, write the SQL.
++++
+++ ## 2) Documentation Policy
+++ 
+++ - Every model must have a `description`.
